Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that uses advanced features like async/await, Promises, generators, and destructuring:

```javascript
// Utility function to simulate async operations
const delay = (ms) => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

// Generator function to produce a sequence of numbers
function* numberGenerator() {
  let num = 1;
  while (true) {
    yield num++;
  }
}

// Async function using the number generator and destructuring
async function processNumbers(generator, limit) {
  const results = [];
  const gen = generator();

  for (let i = 0; i < limit; i++) {
    const { value } = gen.next();
    // Simulate async work
    await delay(100);
    results.push(value * 2);
  }
  
  return results;
}

// Advanced Promise handling
async function main() {
  try {
    const doubledNumbers = await processNumbers(numberGenerator, 5);
    console.log('Processed Numbers:', ...doubledNumbers);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('An error occurred:', error);
  }
}

main();
```

This program involves asynchronous processing of a sequence of numbers generated by a generator, demonstrating destructuring and complex control flow.